00:13:23,040 --> 00:13:26,100
have the obstruction of
urine urine can back up

242
00:13:26,100 --> 00:13:28,660
into the kidney and this
can result in hydronephrosis

243
00:13:28,700 --> 00:13:31,040
so this is something we
really need to be aware

244
00:13:31,040 --> 00:13:34,280
of and look out for and
if we look at the picture

245
00:13:34,280 --> 00:13:37,020
on the top right we can
see the different grades of

246
00:13:37,720 --> 00:13:40,920
hydronephrosis and so depending
on the the obstruction

247
00:13:40,920 --> 00:13:44,820
and how long the obstruction
is Therefore, this

248
00:13:44,820 --> 00:13:47,600
can lead to the different
grades of hydronephrosis.

249
00:13:47,980 --> 00:13:53,220
We can also get an
infection, and this is really

250
00:13:53,220 --> 00:13:57,020
important to treat right
away because kidney stones

251
00:13:57,020 --> 00:14:01,780
that are infected can often
lead to sepsis. We can

252
00:14:01,780 --> 00:14:05,240
also get staghorn colliculi,
which are those large

253
00:14:05,240 --> 00:14:14,740
stones that block the
kidneys and other and it will

254
00:14:14,740 --> 00:14:18,400
not allow urine to pass
through the urinary system.

255
00:14:19,780 --> 00:14:22,820
And kidney failure
can present if we

256
00:14:22,820 --> 00:14:25,700
have staghorn
colliculi bilaterally.

257
00:14:27,140 --> 00:14:31,100
So how do we want to work
up this patient? So first

258
00:14:31,100 --> 00:14:34,220
we want to start with labs.
So important labs we're

259
00:14:34,220 --> 00:14:37,240
going to get are going to
be a basic metabolic panel

260
00:14:37,240 --> 00:14:39,880
and so that's going to help
us assess kidney function

261
00:14:40,120 --> 00:14:42,940
and we're also going to
want to get in a urinalysis.

262
00:14:44,060 --> 00:14:46,460
Urinalysis is going to
help us with two things.

263
00:14:46,460 --> 00:14:48,710
It's going to help us look
for a concurrent infection

264
00:14:49,180 --> 00:14:51,760
and it's going to help
us look for hematuria.

265
00:14:53,740 --> 00:14:56,400
Also a urinalysis
can help us determine

266
00:14:56,400 --> 00:14:59,800
urine pH and this will start to

267
00:15:00,680 --> 00:15:02,760
get us thinking
about what type of

268
00:15:02,760 --> 00:15:04,720
stone this patient
could possibly have.

269
00:15:05,700 --> 00:15:12,180
So a concurrent UTI, we would
see pyuria with positive

270
00:15:12,180 --> 00:15:15,080
nitrites, and we need to
treat this right away.

271
00:15:16,160 --> 00:15:18,120
After we get labs, we're also

272
00:15:18,120 --> 00:15:19,500
going to want to get imaging.

273
00:15:19,840 --> 00:15:22,700
And so the next slide
I'm going to show you,

274
00:15:22,700 --> 00:15:26,160
we see this chart, which
can help us determine

275
00:15:26,160 --> 00:15:28,220
what kind of imaging
would be necessary.

276
00:15:28,740 --> 00:15:32,420
So our imaging options are
going to include a KUB x

277
00:15:32,420 --> 00:15:37,620
-ray, which is a kidney urinary
bladder x-ray. And we also

278
00:15:37,620 --> 00:15:41,840
can do an ultrasound or a
CT scan without contrast.

279
00:15:42,260 --> 00:15:45,380
So normally, we'll start
with an x-ray, and then

280
00:15:45,380 --> 00:15:49,480
we'll get either a CT or
an ultrasound if needed.

281
00:15:49,850 --> 00:15:53,120
One important thing to note
is that if the patient is

282
00:15:53,120 --> 00:15:55,540
pregnant, we're always
going to get an ultrasound

283
00:15:55,540 --> 00:15:58,740
because we don't want to expose
our patient to radiation.

284
00:16:00,860 --> 00:16:04,800
So here are some imaging
clues for kidney stones.

285
00:16:05,100 --> 00:16:07,540
So calcium, struvite,
and cysteine

286
00:16:07,540 --> 00:16:10,200
stones, remember, are
radio-opaque, so they

287
00:16:10,200 --> 00:16:13,380
can be seen on a
KUB x-ray as white.

288
00:16:13,980 --> 00:16:17,320
Uric acid stones are
radiolucent, so they

289
00:16:17,320 --> 00:16:20,600
cannot be seen on an x
-ray, so that's when we

290
00:16:20,600 --> 00:16:22,950
would want to go and
get our non-contrast CT.

291
00:16:23,540 --> 00:16:28,940
These uric acid radiolucine
stones can be caused by

292
00:16:28,940 --> 00:16:32,000
protease inhibitors,
xanthine, or dihydroxyadenine.

293
00:16:32,640 --> 00:16:34,860
Large stones in the
renal pelvic are

294
00:16:34,860 --> 00:16:36,600
usually struvite
stones. These are

295
00:16:36,600 --> 00:16:38,270
going to be your
staghorn colliculi.

296
00:16:40,020 --> 00:16:43,460
Nephrocalcinosis, which is
excessive calcium deposition

297
00:16:43,460 --> 00:16:46,440
in the kidneys, is usually
associated with calcium

298
00:16:46,440 --> 00:16:49,900
phosphate stones and less
often with calcium oxalate

299
00:16:49,900 --> 00:16:53,860
stones. And lastly, if we see
a medullary sponge kidney,

300
00:16:53,860 --> 00:16:56,440
we know that this is
associated with calcium stones.

301
00:16:58,360 --> 00:17:00,960
So I'm going to show
you a couple images of

302
00:17:00,960 --> 00:17:05,920
different x-rays and CT
scans in different stones.

303
00:17:05,920 --> 00:17:08,700
So here we have a KUB,
so remember that's the

304
00:17:08,700 --> 00:17:12,200
kidney urinary bladder x
-ray. And we can see that the

305
00:17:12,200 --> 00:17:15,020
arrow is pointing to a
stone within the ureter.

306
00:17:16,800 --> 00:17:20,760
This is going to be an
image of a non-contrast CT

307
00:17:20,760 --> 00:17:27,560
and we can see that white
circle on the right side of

308
00:17:27,560 --> 00:17:30,500
the image is going to be
a stone within the ureter.

309
00:17:31,860 --> 00:17:34,600
This is also a non
-contrast CT except

310
00:17:34,600 --> 00:17:37,120
this is showing us a
stone in the kidney.

311
00:17:39,460 --> 00:17:42,960
Then we can see this
large stone in the renal

312
00:17:42,960 --> 00:17:45,340
pelvic. This is an
example of a staghorn

313
00:17:45,340 --> 00:17:49,180
calculus, which is most
commonly a struvite stone,

314
00:17:49,360 --> 00:17:53,540
and these are seen in patients
who have chronic UTIs.

315
00:17:56,270 --> 00:17:58,620
This is going to be
a stone that's in the

316
00:17:58,620 --> 00:18:02,480
ureter, and we can see
that above the stone,

317
00:18:02,710 --> 00:18:05,860
the ureter is starting
to swell and get bigger.

318
00:18:06,040 --> 00:18:09,480
so we know that the
stone is obstructing

319
00:18:09,480 --> 00:18:13,840
the ureter and it can
eventually lead to

320
00:18:14,070 --> 00:18:15,380
hydronephrosis and
this is something

321
00:18:15,380 --> 00:18:16,920
we need to immediately treat.

322
00:18:18,120 --> 00:18:21,820
This last image is going
to be an ultrasound

323
00:18:22,160 --> 00:18:25,600
so remember that we
want to do an ultrasound

324
00:18:25,600 --> 00:18:28,300
in patients that are
pregnant because we don't

325
00:18:28,300 --> 00:18:30,630
want to expose them to
excessive radiation.

326
00:18:36,510 --> 00:18:39,190
So now where do stones
commonly cause obstruction,

327
00:18:39,190 --> 00:18:41,830
there's three specific
places that you want to be

328
00:18:41,830 --> 00:18:45,350
aware of. The first is the
ureteral pelvic junction.

329
00:18:45,350 --> 00:18:48,410
So this is where the ureter
meets the renal pelvic.

330
00:18:49,060 --> 00:18:52,610
The next is going to be more
distal. And this is going

331
00:18:52,610 --> 00:18:55,710
to be where the ureter crosses
over the iliac vessels.

332
00:18:55,750 --> 00:18:59,980
And the last place you
want to be aware of is the

333
00:19:02,300 --> 00:19:06,010
uretovesical junction. So
this is where the ureter

334
00:19:06,010 --> 00:19:10,370
meets the bladder and stones
can get launched here too.

335
00:19:12,020 --> 00:19:14,470
So now how do we treat
patients and how do

336
00:19:14,470 --> 00:19:18,570
we manage patients that
have kidney stones?

337
00:19:19,610 --> 00:19:23,450
So first we want to get
those labs and imaging

338
00:19:23,450 --> 00:19:25,850
that we talked about
before. We especially

339
00:19:25,850 --> 00:19:28,850
want to get a non
-contrast CT if our patient

340
00:19:28,850 --> 00:19:31,410
is presenting with a
stone for the first time.

341
00:19:32,490 --> 00:19:35,510
We also want to make sure
we provide our patients

342
00:19:35,510 --> 00:19:38,330
with some kind of pain
management kidney stones

343
00:19:38,330 --> 00:19:40,930
can be really painful
so we want to make sure

344
00:19:40,930 --> 00:19:43,270
that our patients are
comfortable we're going to

345
00:19:43,270 --> 00:19:46,050
start with some kind of
nsaid like catorlac or

346
00:19:46,050 --> 00:19:49,510
ibuprofen and occasionally
we can give opioids for

347
00:19:49,510 --> 00:19:51,690
patients who have
significant renal impairment

348
00:19:52,770 --> 00:19:55,430
we also are going to want
to assess the size of the

349
00:19:55,430 --> 00:19:57,450
stone because this is
going to be helpful to tell

350
00:19:57,450 --> 00:20:00,170
us if a patient will be
able to pass the stone

351
00:20:00,250 --> 00:20:02,950
spontaneously or if we're
going to need to consider

352
00:20:02,950 --> 00:20:05,370
some kind of additional
intervention to help the

353
00:20:05,370 --> 00:20:09,170
patient pass the stone so
for a stone that's less than

354
00:20:09,170 --> 00:20:11,990
or equal to five millimeters
it is likely that the

355
00:20:11,990 --> 00:20:15,710
patient will pass the
stone spontaneously if a

356
00:20:15,710 --> 00:20:18,850
stone is between five and
ten millimeters then there's

357
00:20:18,850 --> 00:20:20,920
a decreased chance that
the stone will pass

358
00:20:20,920 --> 00:20:24,510
however some medications can
be given like alpha blockers

359
00:20:24,510 --> 00:20:28,090
to increase the chance
of spontaneous passage.

360
00:20:28,710 --> 00:20:33,310
So alpha blockers are going
to reduce uterol spasms,

361
00:20:33,310 --> 00:20:36,650
increase pressure proximal
to the stone, and relax

362
00:20:36,650 --> 00:20:39,650
the ureter in the region
of and distal to the stone,

363
00:20:39,650 --> 00:20:42,650
and all this will help
facilitate stone passage.

364
00:20:42,850 --> 00:20:45,890
If the stone is greater
than 10 millimeters,

365
00:20:45,950 --> 00:20:49,190
then it's not likely that
the stone will pass, and

366
00:20:49,190 --> 00:20:51,550
the patient is probably
going to have to undergo

367
00:20:51,550 --> 00:20:54,050
an additional procedure
to remove the stone.

368
00:20:55,280 --> 00:20:57,970
Finally, if we do a
urinalysis and we see a

369
00:20:57,970 --> 00:21:01,770
concurrent UTI, we must
give antibiotics and we

370
00:21:01,770 --> 00:21:04,530
may also want to consider
a urology consult.

371
00:21:06,850 --> 00:21:11,510
So this chart is just
a little information

372
00:21:11,510 --> 00:21:13,890
about whether or
not our patient

373
00:21:13,890 --> 00:21:17,150
should be treated
inpatient or outpatient.

374
00:21:17,430 --> 00:21:21,970
So if our patient is not
able to tolerate their

375
00:21:21,970 --> 00:21:25,270
oral intake, if they have
uncontrolled pain, or

376
00:21:25,270 --> 00:21:27,710
if they have a significant
fever, then we want

377
00:21:27,710 --> 00:21:30,820
to admit our patient for
inpatient management.

378
00:21:32,610 --> 00:21:37,810
Also, if the patient has
signs of a UTI, significant

379
00:21:37,810 --> 00:21:41,110
acute kidney injury,
they're not producing any

380
00:21:41,110 --> 00:21:45,410
urine, or they have
uncontrollable pain, nausea, or

381
00:21:45,410 --> 00:21:49,850
vomiting, we also want to
consider a urology consult.

382
00:21:51,830 --> 00:21:55,470
Outpatient management
is fine if our

383
00:21:55,470 --> 00:21:58,370
patient is able to
keep fluids down,

384
00:21:58,370 --> 00:22:02,250
keep up their volume.
If their pain is

385
00:22:02,250 --> 00:22:03,910
controlled, they
don't have a fever.

386
00:22:04,180 --> 00:22:06,650
And for outpatient
management, we want to provide

387
00:22:06,650 --> 00:22:09,430
them with supportive measures.
So we want to observe,

388
00:22:09,630 --> 00:22:10,710
treat their symptoms,

389
00:22:11,070 --> 00:22:14,530
strain their urine to
see if they've passed

390
00:22:14,530 --> 00:22:17,330
the stone. And we can
also give alpha blockers

391
00:22:17,330 --> 00:22:19,770
like I talked about on
the previous slide to

392
00:22:19,770 --> 00:22:22,830
help with spontaneous
passage of the stone.

393
00:22:24,090 --> 00:22:27,510
So if the stone doesn't
pass after four weeks, if

394
00:22:27,510 --> 00:22:29,970
the patient's in persistent
pain, or if the stone

395
00:22:29,970 --> 00:22:33,350
becomes larger, then we once
again want to consider a

396
00:22:33,350 --> 00:22:37,670
urology consult and consider
additional treatment.

397
00:22:40,210 --> 00:22:43,010
So treatment can
be different based

398
00:22:43,010 --> 00:22:45,450
on what type of stone you have.

399
00:22:45,930 --> 00:22:49,270
So calcium oxalate
stones, we can tell

400
00:22:49,270 --> 00:22:51,630
our patient to have
a low sodium diet.

401
00:22:52,390 --> 00:22:55,970
So low sodium diet can help
reduce the instances of

402
00:22:55,970 --> 00:22:59,390
kidney stones because extra
sodium can cause us to

403
00:22:59,390 --> 00:23:02,450
lose more calcium in the
urine. And this is because

404
00:23:02,450 --> 00:23:07,130
sodium and calcium share
the same transport in

405
00:23:07,130 --> 00:23:10,980
the kidney. So if we have
a high sodium diet, we're

406
00:23:11,050 --> 00:23:14,130
going to be excreting more
sodium into the urine and

407
00:23:14,130 --> 00:23:17,030
this can increase calcium
leakage into the urine

408
00:23:17,630 --> 00:23:20,910
it's also important to
note that low calcium

409
00:23:20,910 --> 00:23:24,130
diet is not recommended
to prevent kidney stones

410
00:23:24,130 --> 00:23:26,870
a low calcium diet
may actually have the

411
00:23:26,870 --> 00:23:29,950
opposite effect and
increase the stone formation

412
00:23:30,430 --> 00:23:34,370
and this is because it
can increase oxalate in

413
00:23:34,370 --> 00:23:37,650
the urine so calcium and
oxalate when consumed

414
00:23:37,650 --> 00:23:41,230
together, they form a
calcium oxalate complex in

415
00:23:41,230 --> 00:23:43,870
the intestinal tract, and
this limits the intestinal

416
00:23:43,870 --> 00:23:46,630
absorption and subsequent
urinary excretion

417
00:23:46,630 --> 00:23:49,990
of free oxalate. However,
if we have a low calcium

418
00:23:49,990 --> 00:23:52,530
diet and we're not
consuming as much calcium,

419
00:23:52,930 --> 00:23:55,850
then free oxalate
becomes increasingly

420
00:23:55,850 --> 00:23:58,870
available for intestinal
absorption, and this

421
00:23:58,870 --> 00:24:02,090
leads to greater urinary
excretion of oxalate

422
00:24:02,390 --> 00:24:05,210
and a higher chance
of stone formation.

423
00:24:05,470 --> 00:24:09,310
So a low-sodium diet is
what we want, but we do not

424
00:24:09,310 --> 00:24:12,050
want to tell our patients
to have a low-calcium diet.

425
00:24:12,270 --> 00:24:15,790
We could also use thiazides
to treat calcium stones,

426
00:24:15,790 --> 00:24:19,770
and thiazides can increase
urinary calcium excretion.

427
00:24:21,630 --> 00:24:26,730
We can treat calcium
phosphate stones with citrate.

428
00:24:26,730 --> 00:24:29,110
We talked about citrate
a little earlier in the

429
00:24:29,110 --> 00:24:31,190
lecture, but citrate is
going to complex with

430
00:24:31,190 --> 00:24:34,450
calcium, which can decrease
urinary calcium levels.

431
00:24:34,770 --> 00:24:38,610
For struvite stones, these
are often associated with

432
00:24:38,610 --> 00:24:42,230
chronic UTIs, so it is important
to treat the underlying

433
00:24:42,230 --> 00:24:44,990
infection if there is
one. And if the stones are

434
00:24:44,990 --> 00:24:48,350
very large, then we want
to surgically remove them.

435
00:24:48,510 --> 00:24:51,550
For uric acid stones,
we want to alkalinize

436
00:24:51,550 --> 00:24:54,510
the urine because
acidic urine increases

437
00:24:54,510 --> 00:24:57,010
our risk of developing
uric acid stones.

438
00:24:57,330 --> 00:25:00,210
And we can use allopurinol.

439
00:25:01,180 --> 00:25:03,850
Allopurinol can reduce
the amount of uric acid in

440
00:25:03,850 --> 00:25:07,090
the urine and help prevent
or treat uric acid stones.

441
00:25:08,300 --> 00:25:10,590
Cysteine, we can
see the low sodium

442
00:25:10,590 --> 00:25:13,330
diet and alkalinization
of the urine.

443
00:25:13,330 --> 00:25:16,330
We can also treat
cysteine stones with

444
00:25:16,330 --> 00:25:18,890
chelating agents because
they can bind the

445
00:25:18,890 --> 00:25:21,210
cysteine and help dissolve
the kidney stones.

446
00:25:24,930 --> 00:25:28,770
Here are some examples of
stone inhibitors. So these

447
00:25:28,770 --> 00:25:31,990
can help prevent stones
from forming by reducing the

448
00:25:31,990 --> 00:25:35,430
initiation of supersaturation
of the urine, crystal

449
00:25:35,430 --> 00:25:38,890
growth, and aggregation rate.
So some examples of stone

450
00:25:38,890 --> 00:25:42,550
inhibitors are going to be
citrate, urea, magnesium,

451
00:25:42,730 --> 00:25:46,250
glycosoaminoglycans,
and pyrophosphate.

452
00:25:46,410 --> 00:25:49,410
And so these stone
inhibitors reduce ionic

453
00:25:49,410 --> 00:25:52,550
activity and the formation
of crystal complexes,

454
00:25:52,550 --> 00:25:55,710
thereby preventing
inhibiting stone formation.

455
00:25:58,290 --> 00:26:00,950
There are also some
indications for

456
00:26:00,950 --> 00:26:02,850
surgical approaches
to be considered

457
00:26:02,850 --> 00:26:04,630
in the treatment
of nephrolithiasis.

458
00:26:06,090 --> 00:26:09,450
So some emergent
indications include stone

459
00:26:09,450 --> 00:26:13,590
obstruction with a concurrent
infection, and this

460
00:26:13,590 --> 00:26:16,340
is because this can
quickly lead to sepsis.

461
00:26:16,870 --> 00:26:20,290
Also, emergent surgery
would be needed if we have

462
00:26:20,290 --> 00:26:24,670
bilateral kidney stones with
acute kidney injury, or if

463
00:26:24,670 --> 00:26:28,510
we have a unilateral
obstruction with acute kidney

464
00:26:28,510 --> 00:26:31,910
injury in those who only
have one functioning kidney.

465
00:26:32,450 --> 00:26:35,650
Some elective indications
to surgical approaches

466
00:26:35,650 --> 00:26:39,010
for the treatment of
nephrolithiasis include large

467
00:26:39,010 --> 00:26:41,590
stones, so stones greater
than 10 millimeters,

468
00:26:42,890 --> 00:26:45,010
uncomplicated distal
stones that are less

469
00:26:45,010 --> 00:26:46,990
than or equal to 10
millimeters that have not

470
00:26:46,990 --> 00:26:49,730
passed in four or more
weeks with medical

471
00:26:49,730 --> 00:26:52,110
management. So this
medical management isn't

472
00:26:52,110 --> 00:26:54,490
working. We need to
consider something else.

473
00:26:54,490 --> 00:26:56,410
If we have symptomatic stones

474
00:26:56,410 --> 00:26:59,010
without any other
etiology for pain,

475
00:26:59,010 --> 00:27:02,210
pregnant patients who
have failed observation,

476
00:27:04,130 --> 00:27:07,150
persistent kidney
obstruction, and then

477
00:27:07,150 --> 00:27:10,610
recurrent UTI-related
stones, so those struvite

478
00:27:10,610 --> 00:27:13,590
stones that can be
sometimes very large.

479
00:27:15,500 --> 00:27:19,110
so there's a couple
different types of surgical

480
